     <div class="sect3">
+     <h4 id="advanced_network_options">
+      2.3.5. Advanced Network Configuration Options
+      <a class="headerlink" href="#advanced_network_options" title="Permalink to this heading">
+      </a>
+     </h4>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       The
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        Advanced Options
+       </span>
+       dialog of the network configuration step provides the
+following settings for the management network.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="dlist">
+      <dl>
+       <dt class="hdlist1">
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         Interface Name Pinning
+        </span>
+       </dt>
+       <dd>
+        <p>
+         Assigns fixed names to the network interfaces, based on their MAC addresses, so
+that the names do not change after kernel or firmware updates, or hardware
+changes that keep the MAC addresses. By default, the interfaces are named
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          nic0
+         </span>
+         ,
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          nic1
+         </span>
+         , and so on, but you can enter other names. To pin the names after
+the installation, for example for a replacement interface with a different MAC
+address, see
+         <a href="#network_override_device_names">
+          Overriding Network Device Names
+         </a>
+         .
+        </p>
+       </dd>
+       <dt class="hdlist1">
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         Management Bond
+        </span>
+       </dt>
+       <dd>
+        <p>
+         Combines two or more of the
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          Bond Interfaces
+         </span>
+         into the bond
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          bond0
+         </span>
+         , which then
+connects the bridge
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          vmbr0
+         </span>
+         to the network instead of a single interface. This
+keeps the host reachable if one of the interfaces or its link fails. The
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          Bond Mode
+         </span>
+         defaults to
+         <em>
+          active-backup
+         </em>
+         , which works without any configuration
+on the switch, while the
+         <em>
+          802.3ad
+         </em>
+         mode requires switch ports configured for
+LACP. The
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          Hash Policy
+         </span>
+         applies to the
+         <em>
+          802.3ad
+         </em>
+         and
+         <em>
+          balance-xor
+         </em>
+         modes, and
+the optional
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          Primary Interface
+         </span>
+         to the
+         <em>
+          active-backup
+         </em>
+         mode, where it is
+preferred as the active interface. See
+         <a href="#sysadmin_network_bond">
+          Linux Bond
+         </a>
+         for a description of the modes.
+        </p>
