+      <div class="paragraph" id="vlan_example_vlan_through_sdn">
+       <div class="title">
+        Example: SDN VLAN Zones with VLAN-aware bridges
+       </div>
+       <p>
+        This is the preferred solution if users have a small number of VLANs inside
+their cluster. On every node, users should configure a VLAN-aware bridge with
+the respective carrier for their VLANs:
+        <a class="headerlink" href="#vlan_example_vlan_through_sdn" title="Permalink to this heading">
+        </a>
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>auto vmbr0
+iface vmbr0 inet manual
+        bridge-ports nic0
+        bridge-stp off
+        bridge-fd 0
+        bridge-vlan-aware yes
+        bridge-vids 2-4094</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        Then create an SDN zone on top of that bridge:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>vlan: vlanz
+        bridge vmbr0
+        ipam pve</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        Then, for each VLAN they want to use, create a VNet:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>vnet: vnet1234
+        zone vlanz
+        tag 1234</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        After applying the SDN configuration, users can then select vnet1234 in the
+network device configuration of their guests. This method does not allow to
+configure an IP for the host directly on a VNet - but it can be done manually
+by adding an entry for the VNet to /etc/network/interfaces:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>auto vnet1234
+iface vnet1234 inet static
+        address 192.0.2.1/24</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
