+     <div class="sect4">
+      <h5 id="vlan_common_issues">
+       Common Issues
+       <a class="headerlink" href="#vlan_common_issues" title="Permalink to this heading">
+       </a>
+      </h5>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <div class="title">
+        Issue: Host not reachable on VLAN interface after guest start
+       </div>
+       <p>
+        A management IP can be configured in a VLAN interface whose underlying
+interface is a bridge port, for example on nic0.5 or bond0.5. If the bridge
+the underlying interface is on is not VLAN-aware, the IP stops working as
+soon as a guest on that bridge and VLAN is started. Stopping the guest will
+not rectify this problem, the host has to be rebooted. To fix this, the VLAN
+interface must be removed, and the management IP configured on the bridge
+VLAN interface, as well as making the bridge VLAN-aware.
+        <a href="#vlan_example_management_ip_and_vlan_aware_bridge">
+         This example
+        </a>
+        shows how to
+do this.
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <div class="title">
+        Issue: ifupdown2 fails after configuring gateway on NIC
+       </div>
+       <p>
+        When configuring a default gateway on the VLAN interface, where the underlying
+interface is part of a VLAN-aware bridge, ifupdown2 will not be able to
+configure the default gateway and fail with a similar error:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>error: nic0.1337: cmd '/bin/ip route replace default via 192.0.2.1 proto kernel
+dev nic0.1337 onlink' failed: returned 2 (Error: Nexthop device is not up.)</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        To avoid this issue, configure the address and default gateway on a VLAN
+interface of the bridge, for example
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         vmbr0.5
+        </span>
+        , as shown in
+        <a href="#vlan_example_management_ip_and_vlan_aware_bridge">
+         this example
+        </a>
+        .
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <div class="title">
+        Issue: Limited number of tags when VLAN offloading to NIC
+       </div>
+       <p>
+        Certain NICs only support VLAN filter offloading for a reduced number of tags.
+In these cases it’s best to either reduce the tag range on the VLAN-aware
+bridge or disable offloading altogether. To determine if offloading is enabled,
+use
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         ethtool
+        </span>
+        , replacing
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         nic0
+        </span>
+        with the name of your interface:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>ethtool -k nic0 | grep rx-vlan-filter</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        If the output contains "[fixed]", the value cannot be changed. Otherwise, use a
+        <span class="monospaced">
+         post-up
+        </span>
+        command to disable offloading, so that the setting persists across
+reboots:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>iface nic0 inet manual
+        post-up ethtool -K nic0 rx-vlan-filter off</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+     </div>
+     <div class="sect4">
+      <h5 id="_troubleshooting">
+       Troubleshooting
+       <a class="headerlink" href="#_troubleshooting" title="Permalink to this heading">
+       </a>
+      </h5>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        There are some helpful commands that can be utilized when debugging such issues:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>$ bridge -compressvlans vlan show</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        This prints a list of bridges and their ports, as well as the VLANs that are
+currently configured for them:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>port              vlan-id
+nic0              1 PVID Egress Untagged
+                  2-4094
+vmbr0             1 PVID Egress Untagged
+veth100i0         1234 PVID Egress Untagged</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>$ bridge fdb show</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        This prints a list of the current forwarding database entries of all bridges.
+The bridge decides how to forward a given packet based on this table. An entry
+of the fdb generally looks like this:
+       </p>
+      </div>
+      <div class="listingblock">
+       <div class="content monospaced">
+        <pre>aa:aa:aa:d8:36:7c dev nic0 vlan 49 master vmbr0 permanent</pre>
+       </div>
+      </div>
+      <div class="paragraph">
+       <p>
+        This means that packets arriving on vmbr0, that have VLAN Tag 49 set and
+destination MAC address aa:aa:aa:d8:36:7c will not be forwarded, but be
+terminated locally.
+       </p>
       </div>
      </div>
     </div>
