-        comment This matcher always matches</pre>
-     </div>
-    </div>
     <div class="sect3">
-     <h4 id="_matcher_options">
-      17.3.1. Matcher Options
-      <a class="headerlink" href="#_matcher_options" title="Permalink to this heading">
+     <h4 id="_match_rules">
+      17.3.1. Match Rules
+      <a class="headerlink" href="#_match_rules" title="Permalink to this heading">
       </a>
      </h4>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Notification matchers can be configured in the Proxmox VE Web UI under Datacenter →
+Notifications. Matchers can be created, modified and deleted from this view.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       When editing a matcher, match rules can be edited under the
+       <em>
+        Match Rules
+       </em>
+       tab.
+Rules are represented as a tree. Each tree node can either match on properties
+of a notification (fields, severity or timestamp) or combine the results of
+child nodes (e.g.
+       <em>
+        All sub-rules match
+       </em>
+       ). Combinator nodes can be nested, which
+makes it possible to compose arbitrarily complex matching behavior.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       The following node types are available:
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="ulist">
+      <ul>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          Always match
+         </strong>
+         : Match any notification.
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          All sub-rules match
+         </strong>
+         : All direct children of this node must match.
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          At least one sub-rule matches
+         </strong>
+         : At least one direct child of this node must
+  match.
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          Exactly one sub-rule matches
+         </strong>
+         : Exactly one direct child of this node must
+  match.
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          Match Field
+         </strong>
+         : Match a metadata field of a notification. This node can either
+  match if the field value matches one of the values provided, or,
+  alternatively, if it matches a regular expression. Available fields
+  are listed in
+         <a href="#notification_events">
+          Notification Events
+         </a>
+         .
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          Match Calendar
+         </strong>
+         : Check if the timestamp of a notification is within a
+  configured time range, based on a selection of weekdays and one contiguous
+  time range within each day (e.g. Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 17:00). If
+  multiple time ranges within a day must be matched, use an
+         <strong>
+          At least one
+  sub-rule matches
+         </strong>
+         node with one
+         <strong>
+          Match Calendar
+         </strong>
+         child per time range.
+        </p>
+       </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         <strong>
+          Match Severity
+         </strong>
+         : Match the severity of a notification. The following
+  severity levels are available:
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          info
+         </span>
+         ,
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          notice
+         </span>
+         ,
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          warning
+         </span>
+         ,
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          error
+         </span>
+         and
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          unknown
+         </span>
+         . Each
+         <strong>
+          Match Severity
+         </strong>
+         node can specify multiple severity levels
+  it matches on, for instance
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          info
+         </span>
+         and
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          notice
+         </span>
+         .
+        </p>
+       </li>
+      </ul>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Each node in the tree can be inverted by checking
+       <strong>
+        Invert Rule
+       </strong>
+       . For instance,
+for a
+       <strong>
+        Match Severity
+       </strong>
+       node matching on
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        info
+       </span>
+       , checking
+       <strong>
+        Invert Rule
+       </strong>
+       lets the
+node match on
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        notice
+       </span>
+       ,
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        warning
+       </span>
+       ,
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        error
+       </span>
+       and
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        unknown
+       </span>
+       .
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Under the
+       <em>
+        Targets to Notify
+       </em>
+       tab, one or more notification targets can be
+selected. All selected targets are notified if a notification is matched.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="sect3">
+     <h4 id="_matcher_configuration_options">
+      17.3.2. Matcher Configuration Options
+      <a class="headerlink" href="#_matcher_configuration_options" title="Permalink to this heading">
+      </a>
+     </h4>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       This section gives an overview of the options used by matchers in the
+configuration file and in the API.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       The following options are used in configuration entries for matchers:
+      </p>
+     </div>
      <div class="ulist">
       <ul>
        <li>
@@ -93846,63 +94803,15 @@
           target
          </span>
          : Determine which target should be notified if the matcher matches.
-  can be used multiple times to notify multiple targets.
+  Can be used multiple times to notify multiple targets.
         </p>
        </li>
        <li>
         <p>
          <span class="monospaced">
-          invert-match
+          disable
          </span>
-         : Inverts the result of the whole matcher
-        </p>
-       </li>
-       <li>
-        <p>
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          mode
-         </span>
-         : Determines how the individual match rules are evaluated to compute
-  the result for the whole matcher.
-  If set to
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          all
-         </span>
-         , all matching rules must match.
-  If set to
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          any
-         </span>
-         , at least one rule must match.
-  Defaults to
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          all
-         </span>
-         .
-        </p>
-       </li>
-       <li>
-        <p>
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          match-calendar
-         </span>
-         : Match the notification’s timestamp against a schedule.
-        </p>
-       </li>
-       <li>
-        <p>
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          match-field
-         </span>
-         : Match the notification’s metadata fields.
-        </p>
-       </li>
-       <li>
-        <p>
-         <span class="monospaced">
-          match-severity
-         </span>
-         : Match the notification’s severity.
+         : Don’t evaluate this matcher.
         </p>
        </li>
        <li>
@@ -93913,224 +94822,670 @@
          : Comment for this matcher.
         </p>
        </li>
+       <li>
+        <p>
+         The matching behavior is defined either by the
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          expression
+         </span>
+         option or by the
+  deprecated options below; the two cannot be combined.
+        </p>
+        <div class="ulist">
+         <ul>
+          <li>
+           <p>
+            Expression-based matchers (preferred):
+           </p>
+           <div class="ulist">
+            <ul>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                expression
+               </span>
+               : Ruleset for this matcher as a single JSON-serialized object
+    (
+               <a href="#notification_match_expression_reference">
+                Match Expression Reference
+               </a>
+               ).
+              </p>
+             </li>
+            </ul>
+           </div>
+          </li>
+          <li>
+           <p>
+            Deprecated matcher options:
+           </p>
+           <div class="ulist">
+            <ul>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                invert-match
+               </span>
+               : Inverts the result of the whole matcher.
+              </p>
+             </li>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                mode
+               </span>
+               : Determines how the individual match rules are evaluated to compute
+    the result for the whole matcher. If set to
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                all
+               </span>
+               , all matching rules must
+    match. If set to
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                any
+               </span>
+               , at least one rule must match. Defaults to
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                all
+               </span>
+               .
+              </p>
+             </li>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-calendar
+               </span>
+               : Match the notification’s timestamp against a schedule.
+    Days can be specified as an inclusive range (e.g.
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                mon..wed
+               </span>
+               ) or enumerated
+    (e.g.
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                mon,wed,sat
+               </span>
+               ). If no days are specified, the time range applies to
+    every day of the week (e.g.
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                08:00-12:00
+               </span>
+               ).
+               <br/>
+               Examples:
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-calendar mon..wed 9-12
+               </span>
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-calendar mon,wed,sat 08:15-12:35
+               </span>
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-calendar 04:15-16:35
+               </span>
+              </p>
+             </li>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-field
+               </span>
+               : Match a notification’s metadata field, either against a list
+    of matched values, or as a regular expression. If the field does not exist,
+    the notification will not be matched.
+               <br/>
+               Examples:
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-field exact:type=vzdump
+               </span>
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-field exact:type=replication,fencing
+               </span>
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-field regex:hostname=^pve[0-9]+$
+               </span>
+              </p>
+             </li>
+             <li>
+              <p>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-severity
+               </span>
+               : Match the notification’s severity.
+               <br/>
+               Examples:
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-severity error
+               </span>
+               <br/>
+               <span class="monospaced">
+                match-severity warning,error,unknown
+               </span>
+              </p>
+             </li>
+            </ul>
+           </div>
+          </li>
+         </ul>
+        </div>
+       </li>
       </ul>
      </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       A matcher without any matching rules always matches; the configured targets will
+always be notified.
+      </p>
+     </div>
+     <div class="paragraph">
+      <p>
+       Starting with Proxmox VE 9.3, match rules created and edited via the UI are stored
+as a single option
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        expression
+       </span>
+       in the respective matcher configuration entry.
+The previously used options (
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        match-field
+       </span>
+       ,
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        match-calendar
+       </span>
+       ,
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        match-severity
+       </span>
+       ,
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        mode
+       </span>
+       and
+       <span class="monospaced">
+        invert-match
+       </span>
+       ) will continue to be supported at least until the
+next major release.
+      </p>
+     </div>
